{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CjyCuGm5ikCYWMZ2RtfmLS","type":"tool_result","content":"doc.go\nentropy.go\ngitleaks.go\ngitleaks_integration_test.go\ngitleaks_test.go\nhelpers_test.go\nhostpath.go\nhostpath_test.go\nmulti.go\npath.go\npath_test.go\nrace_off_test.go\nrace_on_test.go\nredact.go\nredact_test.go\nreplace.go\nrules.go\nscan.go\nscan_test.go\nsecrets.go\nsecrets_test.go\ntruncate.go\ntruncate_test.go\ninternal/redact/helpers_test.go:34:\t\t\"generic-assignment\":  \"api_\" + \"key = \" + randStr(13, 32),\ninternal/redact/rules.go:41:\t// generic-assignment skips unquoted values that are a Go selector or\ninternal/redact/rules.go:44:\t{id: \"generic-assignment\", generic: true,\ninternal/redact/rules.go:51:// generic-assignment already skips values containing \"example\".\ninternal/redact/rules.go:60:// placeholder reports whether a generic-assignment value is an obvious\ninternal/redact/rules.go:62:func placeholder(v []byte) bool {\ninternal/redact/rules.go:75:var codeRefRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(\\.[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)*$`)\ninternal/redact/rules.go:77:// codeRef reports whether v is an unquoted Go selector chain whose segments\ninternal/redact/rules.go:79:func codeRef(v []byte) bool {\ninternal/redact/rules.go:80:\tif !codeRefRE.Match(v) {\n133:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n180:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n256:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is scanned by `scanners/trufflehog-history`. The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n361:5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. The changed lines of the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) are checked against two per-worker-role thresholds. Over `diff_max_lines` (default 3,000) also holds, as `diff guard: N changed lines, limit M`. Over `diff_warn_lines` (default 2,000) but not over the limit, the PR goes ahead: the loop logs `worker.diff_large` (lines, warn, max), and the PR body and the close-out issue comment carry a fixed note, `Large change: N changed lines (warn at W)`, so reviewers know; the note quotes nothing from the change. The latest step's count decides, so a fix round that shrinks the PR drops the note. The comment shows a rejection reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n550:- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ea7b767c-60d2-4f7b-aa96-aa083eb4beec","uuid":"0bf80ccd-192f-41e2-9cbf-01f868d27d63","timestamp":"2026-11-26T08:01:08.913Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"doc.go\nentropy.go\ngitleaks.go\ngitleaks_integration_test.go\ngitleaks_test.go\nhelpers_test.go\nhostpath.go\nhostpath_test.go\nmulti.go\npath.go\npath_test.go\nrace_off_test.go\nrace_on_test.go\nredact.go\nredact_test.go\nrep